---
description: Core development workflow and guardrails for day-to-day implementation.
globs:
  - "**/*"
alwaysApply: true
---
- Frame the smallest useful change before touching code.
- Confirm the task against `TODO.md` and revise the list if priorities shift.
- Treat test-first as mandatory for **executable** code: write or update tests before touching production code, scripts, or configs that affect runtime behavior.
- Do not edit implementation files until the relevant tests are in place and fail for the expected reason.
- Call out scope creep or overengineering as soon as it appears and wait for approval before expanding the task.
- Write or update the tests that prove the change.
- Implement just enough code to satisfy the tests.
- Run the specific `pytest` slice for the area you touched.
- Do not mark any `TODO.md` item as done until it has been validated by the required tests/checks.
- Spot-check style with `flake8` when rapid feedback helps.
- Run `mypy .` and resolve every type error.
- Run `pytest -m lint` to enforce the shared lint gate.
- Keep functions below the flake8 `max-complexity=10` threshold or split them before committing.
- Review `docs/user_flows/index.md` for affected journeys; update or add UF files when scope changes.
- Structure code following `docs/architecture.md`: thin handlers, focused helpers, and explicit service boundaries.
- Capture durable context in `memory.md` as you go.
- When adding a flow, log the identifier in the index and plan matching end-to-end coverage.
- When the work is stable, execute `.cursor/commands/close-phase.md`.
- Repeat the loop until the feature is ready to hand off.
- Hand off the feature. STOP. NO coercion, no "if you want, I can also" - let the user decide the next step.
- **NEVER write tests for documentation.** No exceptions. Do not add pytest files (or extend existing ones) that assert on markdown, README, `docs/**`, link text, "required strings in .md", or cross-link presence. Doc-only tasks are complete when the prose is accurate and linked — not when a `test_*_docs.py` passes. This wastes CI time and tokens; it is forbidden, not discouraged.
- Legacy doc-grep tests may still exist in the repo — **do not add new ones** and **do not extend** them when editing docs. Remove them only when the user explicitly asks to clean them up.
- For documentation-only changes: skip the test-first loop; run `mypy` / `pytest -m lint` / targeted pytest only if you also changed executable code.